From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Danny D. <d3z.the.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 30 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] quickspi: reset touch IC on resume to fix dead touchscreen on
 SP10

On my Surface Pro 10 the touchscreen dies after the device goes to sleep
and comes back, and the only way to get it back is a reboot. It works fine
on 6.14 but breaks on every kernel after that, once the intel-quickspi
driver took over.

The machine only ever suspends through s2idle - /sys/power/mem_sleep shows
"[s2idle]" and has no "deep"/S3 entry - and the touch IC loses power during
that s2idle suspend, the same as it does over hibernation. On resume the
driver only sends a HIDSPI_ON command and restores interrupts/DMA, assuming
the chip kept its state, so the descriptor read fails with "recv failed:
-11" and the touchscreen stays dead. quickspi_restore() (the hibernation
path) already does the right thing and runs a full reset_tic().

This makes resume do the same, but only when the device is not a wake
source: a wake-enabled device keeps its power and state across suspend, so
it stays on the light HIDSPI_ON path and wake-on-touch is not disturbed.
Devices that lose power (like the SP10) go through the full reset.

Tested on my SP10 over a bunch of s2idle suspend/resume cycles, the
touchscreen keeps working now.

Link: https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/issues/1799
Signed-off-by: Danny D. <d3z.the.dev@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid/intel-quickspi/pci-quickspi.c b/drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid/intel-quickspi/pci-quickspi.c
index ad6bd59..d5a6461 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid/intel-quickspi/pci-quickspi.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid/intel-quickspi/pci-quickspi.c
@@ -778,22 +778,73 @@ static int quickspi_resume(struct device *device)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	thc_interrupt_config(qsdev->thc_hw);
+	/*
+	 * If the device is a wake source, its power and state are preserved
+	 * across suspend, so only restore the THC context and power it back
+	 * on. Resetting it here would discard a pending wake touch event and
+	 * break wake-on-touch.
+	 *
+	 * Otherwise the touch IC may have lost power across suspend (e.g. on
+	 * the Surface Pro 10), the same way it does across hibernation, so it
+	 * has to go through the full reset flow to be re-enumerated.
+	 */
+	if (device_may_wakeup(qsdev->dev)) {
+		thc_interrupt_config(qsdev->thc_hw);
+
+		thc_interrupt_enable(qsdev->thc_hw, true);
+
+		ret = thc_dma_configure(qsdev->thc_hw);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		ret = thc_interrupt_quiesce(qsdev->thc_hw, false);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		return quickspi_set_power(qsdev, HIDSPI_ON);
+	}
 
-	thc_interrupt_enable(qsdev->thc_hw, true);
+	thc_spi_input_output_address_config(qsdev->thc_hw,
+					    qsdev->input_report_hdr_addr,
+					    qsdev->input_report_bdy_addr,
+					    qsdev->output_report_addr);
 
-	ret = thc_dma_configure(qsdev->thc_hw);
+	ret = thc_spi_read_config(qsdev->thc_hw, qsdev->spi_freq_val,
+				  qsdev->spi_read_io_mode,
+				  qsdev->spi_read_opcode,
+				  qsdev->spi_packet_size);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = thc_interrupt_quiesce(qsdev->thc_hw, false);
+	ret = thc_spi_write_config(qsdev->thc_hw, qsdev->spi_freq_val,
+				   qsdev->spi_write_io_mode,
+				   qsdev->spi_write_opcode,
+				   qsdev->spi_packet_size,
+				   qsdev->performance_limit);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = quickspi_set_power(qsdev, HIDSPI_ON);
+	thc_interrupt_config(qsdev->thc_hw);
+
+	thc_interrupt_enable(qsdev->thc_hw, true);
+
+	/* The TIC may lose power across system suspend, reset it to recover */
+	ret = reset_tic(qsdev);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	ret = thc_dma_configure(qsdev->thc_hw);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	thc_ltr_config(qsdev->thc_hw,
+		       qsdev->active_ltr_val,
+		       qsdev->low_power_ltr_val);
+
+	thc_change_ltr_mode(qsdev->thc_hw, THC_LTR_MODE_ACTIVE);
+
+	qsdev->state = QUICKSPI_ENABLED;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
